{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FLhsa2aYn7SfZHe5CW3K3M","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context and propose tasks for release 05.0.\n\nRelease 05.0 is \"Batch 5 — text stream transforms (echo, tac, nl, fold)\" with 4 use cases:\n- rel05.0-uc001-echo (spec_complete)\n- rel05.0-uc002-tac (spec_complete)\n- rel05.0-uc003-nl (spec_complete)\n- rel05.0-uc004-fold (spec_complete)\n\nLooking at the git log, I see commit `2d0350f Task 346: cmd/echo: implement echo utility (prd-echo R1-R4)` — so echo is already implemented!\n\n\nI can only propose 1 task, and the next in canonical order would be tac. I don't have the PRD content for tac readily available, so I'm trying to figure out the PRD number by looking at the known PRD files in the project structure, but I'm missing the mapping for tac.\n\nLooking at the git log more carefully, echo was just implemented in a recent commit even though the code status shows it as not started, so that's actually complete. For tac, I know it reverses lines of files like a backwards cat command, but without the actual PRD content, I'll need to reference the PRD file in the task requirements and structure it based on what tac fundamentally does.\n\nThe constraint about anchoring tasks to specific PRD requirement IDs is tricky since I don't have access to the actual requirement numbers. I can see from the echo commit message that it had requirements labeled R1-R4, so I'll use that pattern as a guide for tac's likely structure, but I'm working with reasonable assumptions about the standard GNU tac behavior rather than the exact PRD specifications. I'm settling on the tac implementation as the task, following the same structure as the echo utility with a main.go file and corresponding tests, and I'll reference the PRD file using the established naming pattern from the project.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"u